{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee, one of the surgical team doctors. You've been admitted for severe abdominal pain. Can you tell me what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yeah, the pain in my upper belly’s been getting worse over the past few days. It’s sharp, comes and goes, but lately it hasn’t let up. I’ve also been nauseous and threw up a couple times."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Given your history of chronic pancreatitis, this sounds like another flare-up. We’ll run some tests, but it looks like acute on chronic pancreatitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is it because of those stones again? Last time they said the duct was blocked."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Your recent MRCP showed two stones in the pancreatic duct—one in the neck and one in the mid-body. They’re causing a blockage, and the duct has widened from 4mm to 10mm. That’s likely why the pain is so severe."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what’s the plan? More ERCP? Last time the stent made things worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re not doing another ERCP this time. Because of your history and the size of the stones, the team is planning a Puestow procedure—a surgery to drain the pancreas. It’s scheduled for next week."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Surgery? I thought we could just remove the stones like before."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We could try, but you’ve had multiple ERCPs already, and the stones keep coming back. The Puestow will create a permanent drainage path, which should help prevent future flares."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. What about pain now? It’s really bad."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Right now, you’re getting IV fluids and Dilaudid through a PCA pump—press the button when you need it. We switched from regular IV doses because the PCA gives better control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I pressed it a few times. It’s helping, but not completely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:52:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Give it a little time. We’ll keep monitoring. We also started you on omeprazole to protect your stomach and pancreatic enzymes with meals to help digestion."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:53:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m on those already at home. Should I keep taking duloxetine and lisinopril?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2129-04-29 14:54:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’ve continued all your home meds except painkillers—we’re managing that here. Your blood pressure and mood meds are important to keep stable."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs are stable. The lipase was low on admission, but that’s common in chronic cases. Your glucose is 188, so we’ll keep an eye on it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that high? I have diabetes, but I thought I had it under control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s a bit elevated, but not dangerous. Stress and illness can raise blood sugar. We’re not starting insulin—you’re tolerating sips, and we’ll advance your diet slowly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I eat real food soon? I’m tired of just sipping water."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re planning to move you to clear liquids today. If you tolerate that, tomorrow you can start a low-fat diet. Pancreatitis means we have to go slow with food."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Low-fat, right. I remember from last time. No fried stuff, no heavy meals."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2129-04-30 08:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. And keep taking your pancreatic enzymes with every meal—they help your body digest food properly."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re doing well—no vomiting, pain is better with oral meds now. We’re switching you from the PCA to oral Dilaudid as needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. I don’t like the IV thing anyway. Can I walk around more?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, please do. Ambulating helps prevent blood clots, especially since you have a history of DVT. We’ve been giving you heparin shots and you’ve been using the compression boots—great job."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember that from last hospital stay. I’ll keep walking the hall a few times a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. We also did a chest X-ray today as part of your pre-op workup. It came back normal, which is good before surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is everything looking okay for the surgery next week?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2129-04-30 14:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes. ECG was normal, labs are stable, and you’re moving your bowels and urinating normally. All signs point to you being ready."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—you’re being discharged today. You’ve been on a low-fat diet, pain is controlled with oral meds, and you’re walking well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do before surgery?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Resume all your regular medications except NSAIDs—no ibuprofen, naproxen, or aspirin. They can increase bleeding risk. Take omeprazole, simvastatin, and your other meds as usual."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What about the Dilaudid? I still have some pain at night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re getting a 30-day supply with no refills. Use it only as needed. Also, we’re adding Colace for constipation, which can happen with pain meds."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’ll take it easy, rest, and keep walking. No driving, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. Don’t drive or operate heavy machinery while on pain meds. And avoid lifting anything heavy until your surgeon clears you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. I’ll follow up with my PCP and the surgeon. Is there anything else?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Just stay hydrated, keep eating low-fat, and call if you develop fever, severe pain, or vomiting. Otherwise, see you next week for surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thanks, Doctor. I appreciate all the help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2129-05-01 09:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You’ve done really well. Take care, and we’ll see you soon for the Puestow."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2129-04-29 14:35:00",
    "admission_end": "2129-05-01 15:41:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A 50-year-old male with a history of chronic pancreatitis, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, bipolar disorder, PTSD, and prior alcohol abuse was admitted for acute on chronic pancreatitis due to two obstructing pancreatic duct stones causing ductal dilation. He presented with severe epigastric pain, nausea, and vomiting. Pain was initially managed with IV Dilaudid and transitioned to PCA, then oral medications as symptoms improved. He advanced from sips to a low-fat diet, remained hemodynamically stable, and ambulated throughout the stay. Pre-operative workup including ECG and chest X-ray was completed, and he was discharged home in stable condition to prepare for an upcoming Puestow procedure. Discharge instructions included avoiding NSAIDs, continuing home medications, and follow-up with surgery and primary care.",
    "problems": [
      "Acute pancreatitis",
      "Chronic pancreatitis",
      "Other specified diseases of pancreas",
      "Diabetes with neurological manifestations, type II or unspecified type, not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Gastroparesis",
      "Unspecified essential hypertension",
      "Epilepsy, unspecified, without mention of intractable epilepsy",
      "Alcohol abuse, unspecified",
      "Bipolar disorder, unspecified",
      "Posttraumatic stress disorder",
      "Personal history of venous thrombosis and embolism"
    ]
  }
}
